Since the stickers are identical and the sheets of paper can be reused, we should use the stars and bars method. In this method, we consider the stickers as stars and the dividers between the sheets as bars. With $8$ stickers (or stars) and $3$ dividers (to create $4$ groups), we can calculate the number of ways as:
\begin{eqnarray*}
{8+3 \choose 3}
& = {11 \choose 3} \\
& = \frac{11\cdot 10 \cdot 9}{3!} \\
& = 165
\end{eqnarray*}
The reasoning is contained in the boxed answer, and the final answer is $\boxed{165}$.
